<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hulwan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Holwan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hulwan was an ancient and medieval town at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains, near modern Sarpol-e Zahab in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, where the main road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> climbed onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. An important Sasanian centre, it was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> around 640 and became a frontier town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. It was a seat of the Kurdish Annazid dynasty around 1000 and declined after the Mongol period.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hulwan stood at the entrance of the Paytak Pass. Later Arab tradition, recorded by al-Tabari, made it a foundation of the Sasanian king Kavadh I, but the town was far older: in Assyrian times it was known as Khalmanu and lay on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and Media. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> called it Chala, capital of the district of Chalonitis. Diodorus Siculus derived the name from Greek captives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> settled there, founding the town of Celonae.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> the district was called Khusraw Shad Peroz, the joy of Khusraw the victorious, and the city probably Peroz Kavad. Though it belonged with the rest of Media to the quarter of the North, Khosrau II placed it in the quarter of the West, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, as the kings began to use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> as a summer retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. After the Battle of Qadisiyya in 636 the last Sasanian ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a>, took refuge in Hulwan, leaving it after his defeat at Jalula in 637. The pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under Jarir ibn Abdallah Bajali took the town, garrisoning it with troops that included Persian defectors, the Khamra.</p><p>Until the 10th century Hulwan was a flourishing town on the Khurasan Road, the first town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> province on the way east from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, though it remained fiscally tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawad">Sawad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Under Mu'awiya I it became the capital of western Jibal. Ibn Hawqal found it half the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinavar">Dinavar</a>, with houses of stone and brick and abundant dates, pomegranates and figs; its dried figs were widely exported. Al-Muqaddasi describes a wall with eight gates, a mosque and a synagogue, and between the 8th and 12th centuries it was a metropolitan province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>.</p><p>Around the turn of the 11th century the semi-independent Annazid dynasty governed Hulwan until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakuyids">Kakuyids</a> drove them out. The Seljuq Turks took and burned the town in 1046, and an earthquake in 1049 completed its destruction. It was rebuilt, and its site is now the town of Sarpol-e Zahab, on the road between the Mesopotamian lowlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hulwan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abu'l-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz, chief of the Kurdish Shadhanjan tribe, established himself at <b>Hulwan</b> on the road from Baghdad to Hamadan around 990, as Buyid authority weakened.</blockquote><a href="/state/annazid-dynasty">Annazid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
